## Redundant STx ... ; LDx ...  (This takes every address for memory: a
## hardware register written and read straight back isn't read; cc1 has
## no volatile, and bcc09-copt(1) says so.)

# Exclude indexed with pre/post inc/decrement
ST%1	%2,%[X|Y|U|S]3
LD%1	%2,%[X|Y|U|S]3
=
ST%1	%2,%3

# Exclude indexed and immediate completely
ST%1	%![,|#]%2
LD%1	%![,|#]%2
=
ST%1	%2

## LDx followed by STx doesn't change the flags,
## so no nead for a TSTx instruction to follow...

LD%[A|B]1	%2
ST%[A|B]1	%3
TST%[A|B]1
=
LD%1	%2
ST%1	%3

## LDD #0 is the same as CLRA and CLRB, which are a byte shorter (and a
## cycle slower), except that CLRB clears C: so only before an
## instruction that neither reads C nor follows a branch.  (cc1 reads C
## only right after the instruction that sets it; in the tests and libc,
## no C is read after an LDD #0 before something sets it again.)

LDD	#0
%[STD|LDX|LDY|LDU|PSHS|SUBD|ADDD|TFR|JSR]1	%2
=
CLRA
CLRB
%1	%2

## The LDD #0 above makes CLRA, CLRB: an LDX #0 after them (a long 0, in
## D:X) is TFR D,X, two bytes rather than three, with the same flags
## (CLRB left Z set and N, V and C clear, as LDX #0 would; TFR changes
## none)

CLRA
CLRB
LDX	#0
=
CLRA
CLRB
TFR	D,X

## A 2-byte local given a value as it is pushed: PSHS D (or D and
## more), then a value stored over the D it pushed, is that value pushed
## (D and the flags come out the same).  (Two forms each, as D%1 would
## match DP.)

PSHS	D
CLRA
CLRB
STD	0,S
=
CLRA
CLRB
PSHS	D

PSHS	D,%1
CLRA
CLRB
STD	0,S
=
CLRA
CLRB
PSHS	D,%1

PSHS	D
LDD	#%1
STD	0,S
=
LDD	#%1
PSHS	D

PSHS	D,%1
LDD	#%2
STD	0,S
=
LDD	#%2
PSHS	D,%1

## 0 stored, then 0 loaded again: STD leaves D 0, and the flags as the
## second CLRA, CLRB would (Z set, N and V clear, and C clear from the
## first CLRB)

CLRA
CLRB
STD	%1
CLRA
CLRB
=
CLRA
CLRB
STD	%1

## 0 pushed, then 0 loaded again: PSHS changes neither D nor the flags,
## so the second CLRA, CLRB leaves them as the first did (as after STD,
## above).  (D alone: D,%1 would match DP.)

CLRA
CLRB
PSHS	D
CLRA
CLRB
=
CLRA
CLRB
PSHS	D

## The same for a constant pushed and loaded again: LDD #n sets the
## flags from n both times

LDD	#%1
PSHS	D
LDD	#%1
=
LDD	#%1
PSHS	D

## A branch straight after a return, or after another branch, is never
## run (a label would end the match): cc1 writes a return inside an if
## as the function's exit, then its jump over the else

RTS
%[BRA|LBRA|JMP]1	%2
=
RTS

PULS	%1,PC
%[BRA|LBRA|JMP]2	%3
=
PULS	%1,PC

%[BRA|LBRA]1	%2
%[BRA|LBRA|JMP]3	%4
=
%1	%2

## Every register pushed and pulled straight back does nothing (the
## flags are untouched); as with LEAS 0,S below, the line after is kept

PSHS	D,X,U,Y
PULS	D,X,U,Y
%1
=
%1

## Two LEAS n,S in a row: one, with the sum (the offsets must be numbers:
## copt's arithmetic takes anything else as 0)

LEAS	%![A|B|D]%1,S
LEAS	%![A|B|D]%2,S
=
LEAS	%(%1+%2),S

## TFR X,D then TFR D,X: X and D are already the same (and TFR leaves
## the flags alone)

TFR	X,D
TFR	D,X
=
TFR	X,D

## LEAS 0,S does nothing (the merge above can make it); copt can't
## replace lines with nothing, so this keeps the line after it

LEAS	%(0),S
%1
=
%1
